        {
            "id": "28110404",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "17:05, 13 May 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,037 bytes)",
            "change": -289,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Choose your mason jars depending on how big you want the light to be. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart. The bigger the jar, the bigger your bulb can be and the brighter the light.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy funky, fun bulbs that fit in your jar and match the light kit's specification. Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Then, as long as the bulb fits, you can use any design or light type to fit into your kit.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clean and dry your mason jars with soap and water. Clean them out the best you can, then dry them by hand to prevent water spots from forming. Make sure you get any dust and dirt off the inside of the jar.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Optionally, color your jars before adding bulbs for tinted lights. You can color clear canning jars with food coloring and Elmer's glue. Pour 1 cup of glue into a disposable bowl. Stir in the food coloring that you want to use until it is mixed well. Apply it to the inside of the mason jar with a foam brush. Allow it to dry overnight.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Unscrew the connector from the end of the light kit. This is the big, circular piece the holds the light in place. Take it off, leaving just the cylindrical light fixture at the end of your light kit for now.<reF>https://homeiswhatyoumakeit.com/2012/07/26/diy-mason-jar-pendant-ligh/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Trace the end of the hanging light kit on the top of the jar. Take the end of your hanging light (where the bulb is screwed in) and trace it out on the lid of the mason jar. You'll need to punch through this top to get the light in.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Use a hammer and small nail to punch a hole in the lid somewhere on your line. This is the simplest set of tools to use, but you can also use a power drill if you have one on hand. Punch a small hole into the lid using the nail or your drill, then remove the nail.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Continue punching holes around the circle until you've \"cut\" out the hole for the light. Use your hammer and nail to keep working around your traced circle until it falls out.<reF>http://woonblog.typepad.com/woonblog/2012/10/diy-bokaallamp.html</reF><br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Thread the light fixture into the hole in your lid, then screw the connector back on. Make sure that the wire runs out of the jar and the light fixture points into it. If you need, use the back of your hammer to bend back any sharp or rough edges in the jar. The connector, however, should keep the whole light fixture in place on the lid.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Optionally, use modeling clay or super glue to close the seam between the light fixture and the lid. While few people will notice a rough cut on the top of a light, you can tighten this connection easily with a hard, quick-drying adhesive. While it will not affect the light much, it can prevent the fixture from wiggling around if your cut isn't snug.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 11.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 11-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 11 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Screw your light bulb into the light fixture, then screw the top into the jar. Turn it on to make sure it works, and voila!<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 3.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12-preview Version 3.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>{{expertgreenbox:161062|'''Expert Trick''': For an easy alternative, place some battery-powered fairy lights inside of a mason jar! You can change the color of the light by painting the jar with some translucent paint. You can also try coating the jar with a layer of Mod Podge dyed with food coloring.|}}[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Choose your mason jars depending on how big you want the light to be. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart. The bigger the jar, the bigger your bulb can be and the brighter the light.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy funky, fun bulbs that fit in your jar and match the light kit's specification. Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Then, as long as the bulb fits, you can use any design or light type to fit into your kit.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clean and dry your mason jars with soap and water. Clean them out the best you can, then dry them by hand to prevent water spots from forming. Make sure you get any dust and dirt off the inside of the jar.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4-preview Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Optionally, color your jars before adding bulbs for tinted lights. You can color clear canning jars with food coloring and Elmer's glue. Pour 1 cup of glue into a disposable bowl. Stir in the food coloring that you want to use until it is mixed well. Apply it to the inside of the mason jar with a foam brush. Allow it to dry overnight.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make-Mason-Jar-Lights-Step-6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Unscrew the connector from the end of the light kit. This is the big, circular piece the holds the light in place. Take it off, leaving just the cylindrical light fixture at the end of your light kit for now.<reF>https://homeiswhatyoumakeit.com/2012/07/26/diy-mason-jar-pendant-ligh/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Trace the end of the hanging light kit on the top of the jar. Take the end of your hanging light (where the bulb is screwed in) and trace it out on the lid of the mason jar. You'll need to punch through this top to get the light in.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7-preview Version 2.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use a hammer and small nail to punch a hole in the lid somewhere on your line. This is the simplest set of tools to use, but you can also use a power drill if you have one on hand. Punch a small hole into the lid using the nail or your drill, then remove the nail.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Continue punching holes around the circle until you've \"cut\" out the hole for the light. Use your hammer and nail to keep working around your traced circle until it falls out.<reF>http://woonblog.typepad.com/woonblog/2012/10/diy-bokaallamp.html</reF><br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Thread the light fixture into the hole in your lid, then screw the connector back on. Make sure that the wire runs out of the jar and the light fixture points into it. If you need, use the back of your hammer to bend back any sharp or rough edges in the jar. The connector, however, should keep the whole light fixture in place on the lid.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Optionally, use modeling clay or super glue to close the seam between the light fixture and the lid. While few people will notice a rough cut on the top of a light, you can tighten this connection easily with a hard, quick-drying adhesive. While it will not affect the light much, it can prevent the fixture from wiggling around if your cut isn't snug. [[Image:Make-Mason-Jar-Lights-Step-10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Screw your light bulb into the light fixture, then screw the top into the jar. Turn it on to make sure it works, and voila!<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]{{expertgreenbox:161062|'''Expert Trick''': For an easy alternative, place some battery-powered fairy lights inside of a mason jar! You can change the color of the light by painting the jar with some translucent paint. You can also try coating the jar with a layer of Mod Podge dyed with food coloring.|}}"
                ]
            }
        },
