            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]{{expertgreenbox:161062|'''Try string lights for a simple alternative.''' Claire Donovan-Blackwood, owner of Heart Handmade UK, says: \"The easiest way to make a mason jar light would be to take really thin strands of lights, then wrap them up and stick them in a dyed jar. Even dyeing the jar is easy, because all you have to do is to coat the jar with a little Mod Podge dyed with food coloring. That way, you can make the lights any color you want!\"|}}",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26977690",
            "user": "Hapayne",
            "timestamp": "18:23, 22 August 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,018 bytes)",
            "change": -454,
            "summary": "(Updated green box.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]{{expertgreenbox:161062|'''Try string lights for a simple alternative.''' Claire Donovan-Blackwood, owner of Heart Handmade UK, says: \"The easiest way to make a mason jar light would be to take really thin strands of lights, then wrap them up and stick them in a dyed jar. Even dyeing the jar is easy, because all you have to do is to coat the jar with a little Mod Podge dyed with food coloring. That way, you can make the lights any color you want!\"|}}"
                ]
            }
        },
