                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy funky, fun bulbs that fit in your jar and match the light kit's specification. Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Then, as long as the bulb fits, you can use any design or light type to fit into your kit.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clean and dry your mason jars with soap and water. Clean them out the best you can, then dry them by hand to prevent water spots from forming. Make sure you get any dust and dirt off the inside of the jar.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4-preview.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Unscrew the connector from the end of the light kit. This is the big, circular piece the holds the light in place. Take it off, leaving just the cylindrical light fixture at the end of your light kit for now.<reF>https://homeiswhatyoumakeit.com/2012/07/26/diy-mason-jar-pendant-ligh/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Trace the end of the hanging light kit on the top of the jar. Take the end of your hanging light (where the bulb is screwed in) and trace it out on the lid of the mason jar. You'll need to punch through this top to get the light in.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use a hammer and small nail to punch a hole in the lid somewhere on your line. This is the simplest set of tools to use, but you can also use a power drill if you have one on hand. Punch a small hole into the lid using the nail or your drill, then remove the nail.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Continue punching holes around the circle until you've \"cut\" out the hole for the light. Use your hammer and nail to keep working around your traced circle until it falls out.<reF>http://woonblog.typepad.com/woonblog/2012/10/diy-bokaallamp.html</reF><br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Thread the light fixture into the hole in your lid, then screw the connector back on. Make sure that the wire runs out of the jar and the light fixture points into it. If you need, use the back of your hammer to bend back any sharp or rough edges in the jar. The connector, however, should keep the whole light fixture in place on the lid.<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Screw your light bulb into the light fixture, then screw the top into the jar. Turn it on to make sure it works, and voila!<br><br>{{whvid|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12.360p.mp4|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12-preview.jpg|Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Choose your mason jars depending on how big you want the light to be. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart. The bigger the jar, the bigger your bulb can be and the brighter the light.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy funky, fun bulbs that fit in your jar and match the light kit's specification. Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Then, as long as the bulb fits, you can use any design or light type to fit into your kit.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>",
                    "#Clean and dry your mason jars with soap and water. Clean them out the best you can, then dry them by hand to prevent water spots from forming. Make sure you get any dust and dirt off the inside of the jar. [[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Unscrew the connector from the end of the light kit. This is the big, circular piece the holds the light in place. Take it off, leaving just the cylindrical light fixture at the end of your light kit for now.<reF>https://homeiswhatyoumakeit.com/2012/07/26/diy-mason-jar-pendant-ligh/</ref>",
                    "#Trace the end of the hanging light kit on the top of the jar. Take the end of your hanging light (where the bulb is screwed in) and trace it out on the lid of the mason jar. You'll need to punch through this top to get the light in.",
                    "#Use a hammer and small nail to punch a hole in the lid somewhere on your line. This is the simplest set of tools to use, but you can also use a power drill if you have one on hand. Punch a small hole into the lid using the nail or your drill, then remove the nail.",
                    "#Continue punching holes around the circle until you've \"cut\" out the hole for the light. Use your hammer and nail to keep working around your traced circle until it falls out.<reF>http://woonblog.typepad.com/woonblog/2012/10/diy-bokaallamp.html</reF>",
                    "#Thread the light fixture into the hole in your lid, then screw the connector back on. Make sure that the wire runs out of the jar and the light fixture points into it. If you need, use the back of your hammer to bend back any sharp or rough edges in the jar. The connector, however, should keep the whole light fixture in place on the lid.",
                    "#Screw your light bulb into the light fixture, then screw the top into the jar. Turn it on to make sure it works, and voila!",
                    "#Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "20140753",
            "user": "Ngeisler",
            "timestamp": "19:12, 2 May 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,202 bytes)",
            "change": 328,
            "summary": "(edits for accuracy/helpfulness, prepping for video)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "===Rounding Up Supplies===",
                    "#Choose your mason jars depending on how big you want the light to be. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart. The bigger the jar, the bigger your bulb can be and the brighter the light.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*For the average bulb (60 watts), a normal 12-ounce jar should be perfect.",
                    "#Find a pendant or hanging light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. Ikea, in particular, sells a kit designed for simple DIY projects like this, but other retailers should have kits as well. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy funky, fun bulbs that fit in your jar and match the light kit's specification. Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Then, as long as the bulb fits, you can use any design or light type to fit into your kit.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>",
                    "#Clean and dry your mason jars with soap and water. Clean them out the best you can, then dry them by hand to prevent water spots from forming. Make sure you get any dust and dirt off the inside of the jar. [[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Optionally, color your jars before adding bulbs for tinted lights. You can color clear canning jars with food coloring and Elmer's glue. Pour 1 cup of glue into a disposable bowl. Stir in the food coloring that you want to use until it is mixed well. Apply it to the inside of the mason jar with a foam brush. Allow it to dry overnight.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>",
                    "#*You can also use decoupage glue; just be certain that whatever glue you use is easy to apply and dries clear.",
                    "#*You can also spray paint your lid and light fixtures to match if you want a more elegant light. Try a copper, silver, or gold spray paint.<reF>http://www.designsponge.com/2008/06/diy-project-whitneys-mason-jar-lights.html</ref>",
                    "",
                    "===Building the Light===",
                    "#Unscrew the connector from the end of the light kit. This is the big, circular piece the holds the light in place. Take it off, leaving just the cylindrical light fixture at the end of your light kit for now.<reF>https://homeiswhatyoumakeit.com/2012/07/26/diy-mason-jar-pendant-ligh/</ref>",
                    "#Trace the end of the hanging light kit on the top of the jar. Take the end of your hanging light (where the bulb is screwed in) and trace it out on the lid of the mason jar. You'll need to punch through this top to get the light in.",
                    "#*Make sure you're using both parts of the lid -- the round, flat piece and the circular \"cuff\" that screws on.",
                    "#Use a hammer and small nail to punch a hole in the lid somewhere on your line. This is the simplest set of tools to use, but you can also use a power drill if you have one on hand. Punch a small hole into the lid using the nail or your drill, then remove the nail.",
                    "#*Punch along the inside of your tracing line. You'd rather make it a bit too small at first than too large.",
                    "#Continue punching holes around the circle until you've \"cut\" out the hole for the light. Use your hammer and nail to keep working around your traced circle until it falls out.<reF>http://woonblog.typepad.com/woonblog/2012/10/diy-bokaallamp.html</reF>",
                    "#*If you have a pair of tin cutters or strong scissors, you can use these to speed up the process.<reF>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLwqns5pgjE</ref>",
                    "#Thread the light fixture into the hole in your lid, then screw the connector back on. Make sure that the wire runs out of the jar and the light fixture points into it. If you need, use the back of your hammer to bend back any sharp or rough edges in the jar. The connector, however, should keep the whole light fixture in place on the lid.",
                    "#*If the hole is a little small, push and twist to try and get it through and keep a nice tight seal between the light and lid.",
                    "#Optionally, use modeling clay or super glue to close the seam between the light fixture and the lid. While few people will notice a rough cut on the top of a light, you can tighten this connection easily with a hard, quick-drying adhesive. While it will not affect the light much, it can prevent the fixture from wiggling around if your cut isn't snug.",
                    "#Screw your light bulb into the light fixture, then screw the top into the jar. Turn it on to make sure it works, and voila!",
                    "#Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Aim to install your hooks in a ceiling beam. They can fall if they are simply screwed into plaster.<reF>http://www.designsponge.com/2008/06/diy-project-whitneys-mason-jar-lights.html</reF>",
                    "#*You can also make 2 or 3 similar pendant lights and knot the chords together. Hang them from 1 sturdy ceiling hook to create a larger light fixture.",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<reF>http://mountainmodernlife.com/25-creative-ways-to-light-up-mason-jars/</ref>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Find a pendant light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Choose a low wattage, around 60 watts for small mason jar lights and buy the appropriate bulbs.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Choose your mason jars. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* The size of the canning jar you choose will determine the size of the light fixture and how heavy it will be. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Remove the lids from your canning jars. Set them on a work table so that you can create a hole for your light fixture.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Clean your mason jars. Wash them in the dishwasher or with soap and water. At this time, you can also choose to color the jars to match your décor.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You can color clear canning jars with food coloring and Elmer's glue. You can also use decoupage glue; just be certain that whatever glue you use is easy to apply and dries clear. Pour 1 cup of glue into a disposable bowl. Stir in the food coloring that you want to use until it is mixed well. Apply it to the inside of the mason jar with a foam brush. Allow it to dry overnight.",
                    "# Clamp your mason jar lids to your work table. Put on your safety gear, such as work gloves, safety glasses and a long-sleeved shirt.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Place your round light socket on the top of the mason jar lid. Draw around in a circle with a marker. This will serve as a guide to help you to cut the right shape in the top.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Repeat this with each mason jar lid you plan to use for a pendant light.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7Bullet1.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Place an arbor drill bit into your electric drill. This is a large drill bit that will help you to take the center of the lid out in an even circle. Choose an arbor drill bit that coincides with the size of your light socket.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Drill into the top of the mason jar. Remove the inside of the drawn circle. Make the hole slightly smaller, instead of larger than the circle, so that the socket will fit snugly.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you do not own an electric drill, take a hammer and nail and hammer many holes around the marked circle. When the area around the circle is weakened, peel off the top and cut it with a pair of shears or scissors. Make sure to wear thick gloves while cutting and peeling the metal lid.",
                    "# Place the light socket through the hole in the lid. The area where the light is screwed in should protrude through the bottom of the lid. The cord should extend upward.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Make sure the socket is snug before continuing further in the process. If it is too snug, use pliers to bend a portion of the metal to make more room.",
                    "# Screw the bulb into the socket. Test the light by plugging the chord into your electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Place the lid on the mason jar, being careful not to knock the bulb against the glass. Turn the jar to screw the lid on securely.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Aim to install your hooks in a ceiling beam. They can fall if they are simply screwed into plaster.",
                    "#* You can also make 2 or 3 similar pendant lights and knot the chords together. Hang them from 1 sturdy ceiling hook to create a larger light fixture.",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "* Electrician",
                    "* Electric drill",
                    "* Clamps",
                    "* Arbor drill bit",
                    "* Clear drying glue",
                    "* Food coloring",
                    "* Paintbrush",
                    "",
                    "* [http://theycallmedomesticate.com/2012/07/how-to-make-mason-jar-lights-diy-string-light/ http://theycallmedomesticate.com/2012/07/how-to-make-mason-jar-lights-diy-string-light/]",
                    "* [http://snapguide.com/guides/make-a-mason-jar-light/ http://snapguide.com/guides/make-a-mason-jar-light/]",
                    "* [http://in.gredients.com/2012/06/01/how-to-make-mason-jar-lights/ http://in.gredients.com/2012/06/01/how-to-make-mason-jar-lights/]",
                    "* [http://www.curbly.com/users/capreek/posts/10187-roundup-15-awesome-and-easy-diy-mason-jar-projects http://www.curbly.com/users/capreek/posts/10187-roundup-15-awesome-and-easy-diy-mason-jar-projects]"
                ]
            }
        },
