                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Find a pendant light kit from your local hardware store. These kits are commonly sold in order to light paper or other lanterns. They can be hung from a hook, strung along ceilings or walls and plugged into an electrical outlet.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Look at the wattage requirements for the light kit. Each socket should specify what watts are required. Choose a low wattage, around 60 watts for small mason jar lights and buy the appropriate bulbs.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Choose your mason jars. You can find vintage mason jars at antique stores or find canning jars online or in kitchen supply stores. In some locations, they sell canning jars by the dozen at supermarkets.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* The size of the canning jar you choose will determine the size of the light fixture and how heavy it will be. Canning jars come in varying sizes, including 8 oz., 1 pint and 1 quart.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 3Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Remove the lids from your canning jars. Set them on a work table so that you can create a hole for your light fixture.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Clean your mason jars. Wash them in the dishwasher or with soap and water. At this time, you can also choose to color the jars to match your décor.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Clamp your mason jar lids to your work table. Put on your safety gear, such as work gloves, safety glasses and a long-sleeved shirt.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Place your round light socket on the top of the mason jar lid. Draw around in a circle with a marker. This will serve as a guide to help you to cut the right shape in the top.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Repeat this with each mason jar lid you plan to use for a pendant light.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 7Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Place an arbor drill bit into your electric drill. This is a large drill bit that will help you to take the center of the lid out in an even circle. Choose an arbor drill bit that coincides with the size of your light socket.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Drill into the top of the mason jar. Remove the inside of the drawn circle. Make the hole slightly smaller, instead of larger than the circle, so that the socket will fit snugly.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Place the light socket through the hole in the lid. The area where the light is screwed in should protrude through the bottom of the lid. The cord should extend upward.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Screw the bulb into the socket. Test the light by plugging the chord into your electrical outlet.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Place the lid on the mason jar, being careful not to knock the bulb against the glass. Turn the jar to screw the lid on securely.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Place hooks in the ceiling where you want your pendant lamp to hang. If your pendant light cord is covered in thick plastic, you may be able to knot it and hang the light from a hook from the knot for more support.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Call an electrician to install pendant lights within your ceiling or walls. If you want your mason jar lights to hang directly from the ceiling, you should not try to install them on your own, unless you have extensive experience with electrical wiring. Alternatively, you can string the cord along your wall or ceiling, outside of the wall plaster, and plug it into an electrical outlet.<br><br>[[Image:Make Mason Jar Lights Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
